---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";
// Course study, W25/26 "Foundations in Psychology and Empirical Study Design" (UTN).
// Facts from the Vault record verified on 19 Sep 2026 (A2u1): design and protocol by a
// group of three, adopted for the class-wide study by class vote; XC did the analysis.
// No names of teammates or the instructor; no per-group numbers beyond N (class data,
// descriptive only). Added at XC's request, 28 Sep 2026.
const p = projects.psyStudy;
---
<Base title={`${p.title} · Xiaochuan Li`}
  description="A between-subjects course experiment on whether meeting ChatGPT first changes how people check and double-check answers: design, pilot, revision, online data collection (N = 49), and analysis."
  toc={true}>
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="!mb-6 !text-sm"><a href="/research#projects">← Course projects</a></p>
    <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">{p.status}</p>
    <h1>{p.title}</h1>
    <p class="!text-lg">{p.question}</p>
    <p>A study from the course Foundations in Psychology and Empirical Study Design at the
      University of Technology Nuremberg. Our group of three wrote the protocol; the class voted
      to adopt it as the study everyone would run, and the whole class collected the data.</p>

    <h2>My contribution</h2>
    <p>I co-designed the study with two classmates and did the data analysis on my own:
      descriptive statistics, frequency plots, and group comparisons in JASP.</p>

    <h2>Design</h2>
    <p>Participants answered factual questions and could check their answers.
      The first checking tool they were offered was assigned at random, Wikipedia or ChatGPT,
      between subjects. Their behaviour over two rounds was coded as one of four paths: whether they
      checked an answer, and whether they then double-checked it with another source. Each answer also carried a
      four-point certainty rating, and participants rated how often they found ChatGPT and
      Wikipedia to be right.</p>
    <p>Habitual double-checking was asked only after the task, so that the question would not
      reveal what the study was about. Participants gave informed consent online; the task
      used a mild cover story, followed by a debriefing. The experiment ran
      in PsychoPy and online through Pavlovia.</p>

    <h2>Pilot and revision</h2>
    <p>A pilot with eight participants showed that the online group assignment was not working:
      six of the eight landed in the same group. We fixed the assignment. Participants also
      answered the questions too well to need checking, so two items were replaced with harder
      ones; one item that a participant flagged as culturally biased was replaced; and an
      authority rating for Wikipedia was added alongside the one for ChatGPT.</p>

    <h2>Results and limits</h2>
    <p>The main study had 49 participants. The descriptive pattern matched one of the two
      predictions and not the other. The course covered descriptive analysis only, so these
      are observed patterns, not tested effects; the groups were also unequal in size.</p>

    <h2>Methods and scope</h2>
    <p>Between-subjects design, random assignment, rating scales, informed consent and
      debriefing, pilot testing, PsychoPy and Pavlovia, descriptive analysis in JASP.
      This was a completed course study; my individual part was the analysis.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
